From 8cb6ba9d8471657c05488c67faf7ae1908d76e7f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Armada <noreply@armada.local>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(ssc): keep retrying until the SSC answers

The SSC usually comes up after InputPlumber starts, and the source only
tried to open the sensors once, so gyro stayed off until something
restarted InputPlumber. Open them from poll() and retry whichever is still
closed every 2 s. An open sensor is kept, since libssc callbacks may still
reference it.

Each failed libssc open leaks its QRTR socket and name-service lookup, and
retrying through it hits the kernel's per-node lookup limit, after which no
QRTR lookup on the system works. So ask the name service for the SSC with a
short-lived socket first, and only open through libssc once it is listed.

Sensor creation was cancelled after 1 s and open after 4 s. Client setup
can take longer than 1 s, so creation failed sensors that would have come
up, and libssc's late completion then used the freed init task; its open
path frees its task on cancel the same way. Let libssc's own timeouts apply
instead.

A FastRPC node created after InputPlumber starts was looked up in udev's
fastrpc subsystem, but udev files it under misc, so the SSC source never
appeared when the ADSP came up late.

Retrying also made the GError leak matter: free them. A sensor whose open
failed is deliberately not released, since libssc keeps a report callback
on it. Treat a FALSE return from open as a failure even when no GError is
set.
